    "case_id": "713",
    "case_summary": "A 30-year-old male presented with a 6-year history of recurrent respiratory symptoms and acute exacerbation with dyspnea for 1 day. Past medical history included hospitalization in intensive care unit one year ago requiring mechanical ventilation for over 10 days. Prior pulmonary function tests showed severe mixed ventilation dysfunction with FEV1 PreD of 26% and FEV1/FVC percent of 56%. Patient had previously scored 9 on the Asthma Control Test and 6 on the Asthma Control Questionnaire.\n\nVital signs on admission:- Temperature: 35.5°C- Pulse: 121 beats/min- Respiratory rate: 32 times/min- Blood pressure: 169/100 mmHg- SpO2: 83% (without oxygen)Physical findings:- Orthopnea- Cyanosis of the lips- Profuse sweating- Three-concave sign- Hyperresonance in percussion of both lungs- Accelerated respiration- Loud and diffuse wheezing rales in both lungs\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Blood gas analysis: pH 7.137, PCO2 74.7 mmHg, PO2 95.0 mmHg, SO2 94.0%, FiO2 41.0%, oxygenation index 232 mmHg- Complete blood count: WBC 15.51 × 109/L, neutrophil percentage 74.2%, eosinophil percentage 7.2%- Other biochemical and coagulation parameters were normalImaging Studies:- Chest CT  Title: Chest CT examination  Description: No major abnormalities observed in both lungs and heartPulmonary Function Tests:- Bronchodilator test: increase in absolute FEV1 of 250 mL, improvement rate of 24%- Follow-up tests showed FEV1: 2.2L, FVC: 3.77L, FEV1%pred: 56.9%, FEV1/FVC%: 69.8%, PEF: 5.88L/s",
